  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{-x}=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{-x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({5}^{-1})}^{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943873/assets/0f9cf17608e32b91f4efbb243702286b484938c015f74f0b59ca906dc24c593a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$",
